n surprise leading to hawkish repricing could cause policy-driven nominal move that lifts breakevens despite expectations hold."}], "trade": {"flat": true, "legs": [], "conviction": 0.55, "rationale": "Breakevens narrowing depends critically on expectations staying anchored as realized inflation moderates from recent highs. Current positioning is crowded long and the three-week flatness is not yet conclusive. Holding flat rather than short avoids duration/policy risk mismatch."}}

{"notes": "Inflation has spiked sharply higher in realised terms, with headline CPI now at 5.25% year-on-year and core PCE at 3.93%, both driven by a material pickup in monthly momentum. The 3-month annualized headline rate sits at 8.17%, well above the 5.70% core annualized, reflecting a temporary goods surge rather than persistent disinflation. However, the critical asymmetry lies in expectations: the 10-year breakeven has barely moved, hovering at 2.40% and below its 12-month high of 2.54%. Over the past three months, breakevens have risen just 8bp despite headline inflation accelerating by 111bp year-on-year. This is the classic "hot realised, anchored expectations" setup.

The labour market shows meaningful tightening: unemployment has fallen 70bp in three months to 5.4%, sitting at the bottom of its 12-month range and only 140bp above estimated natural rate. This tightness will likely sustain the momentum in nominal wages and price-setting behaviour, supporting the inflation persistence case. Yet labour itself is not generating a fresh upside surprise—the jobless rate has simply worked through its cyclical reversion. The Fed's balance sheet has expanded substantially (up 440bn in 13 weeks, 5.66% over the year), providing monetary accommodation that should support demand and price pressure.

