{"notes": "Inflation realised is cooling but with tactical uncertainty. Headline CPI is at 4.95% y/y, down sharply from the 8.98% peak and near the 4.92% twelve-month low; the three-month annualized is now 3.27%, a full 140bp below the year-ago level. Core PCE at 4.78% is sticky but retreating, with core's three-month annualized at 4.74% versus 5.59% a year ago. The most recent month-over-month headline print was 0.34%, but the outgoing print was 0.95%, suggesting some residual momentum risk before the next release.

Inflation expectations, however, have softened considerably and are now anchored well below the realised rate. The ten-year breakeven is at 2.18%, down 16bp in three weeks and 67bp over the past year; it is 88bp above the twelve-month low of 2.10% but sits comfortably within the narrow range. The market is pricing in medium-term inflation well below both current realised rates and the Fed's target, a marked divergence from the persistence signals embedded in wage-sensitive labour tightness.

